extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Accessibility"

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Accessibility
				p.lead What screen readers read, and what works without colour, motion or JavaScript.

	+section("Screen readers read the table")
		p The table stays in the page. It is hidden from sight, but screen readers read it like any other table: the names of the rows and columns, and every number.
		p The drawing is hidden from screen readers, with #[code aria-hidden="true"]. That includes the numbers and names in the drawing, because the table already has them. So a screen reader does not read everything twice. The names under the chart and the legend are also #[code inert], because Safari with VoiceOver reads them otherwise.
		p Give the chart a title with a #[code figcaption]. Tablechart makes it the name of the figure and of the table, with #[code aria-labelledby]. So a screen reader that jumps to the table also reads the title and the unit. If the figure or the table already has a name, with #[code aria-label], #[code aria-labelledby] or a #[code caption], it keeps that name.
		p A screen reader does not read a line break. So Tablechart puts a full stop before each #[code br] in the title, hidden from sight, and a screen reader pauses between the title and the subtitle. If the line already ends with a full stop, a question mark, a colon or a comma, nothing is added.

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<figure data-chart="column">
				<figcaption><b>Apples picked</b><br><span>thousands</span></figcaption>
				<table>…</table>
			</figure>

		p If the chart has no #[code figcaption], add #[code aria-label] to the figure, such as #[code aria-label="Apples picked, in thousands"].

	+section("The total of a donut")
		p If a donut has no total row, the number in the middle is the sum of the slices. Tablechart adds it to the table, in a hidden row at the bottom, so screen readers read it with a name: "Total, 74.5". The #[code totallabel] option sets the name.

	+section("Annotations")
		p The text of an annotation, such as #[code +24%] on a bracket, stays in the page under the table, hidden from sight. Screen readers read it there. The callout in the drawing is hidden from them.

	+section("Without JavaScript")
		p If JavaScript does not run, there is no chart. The table is then shown instead, with its title.
		p A chart with #[code data-chart-src] has no table until JavaScript has read the JSON file. If JavaScript does not run, or the file cannot be read, there is no table and no chart. If the chart must work without JavaScript, write the table in the HTML.

	+section("Colour")
		ul
			li Lines are told apart by their points as well: filled and open circles, squares and diamonds. You can also make a line dashed or dotted. See #[a(href=url('/types/line.html')) Line].
			li A number inside a bar is dark or light, whichever reads on the colour of the bar.
			li If you set your own colours, check that they contrast enough with the background: 3:1 for bars and lines, and 4.5:1 for a number inside a bar.

	+section("Zoom and text size")
		p All text is HTML, sized in #[code em]. It follows the text size and the zoom of the browser, and stays sharp. Long names beside the bars of a bar chart wrap onto more lines.

	+section("Motion")
		p If the visitor has asked for reduced motion in their system settings, charts are shown without animation.

	+section("Numbers that do not fit")
		p If there is no room for a number, it is left out of the drawing. It is still in the table, so screen readers read it. Pointing at the row, or tapping it, shows the number.
		p Showing a number this way does not work with the keyboard yet.

	+section("Language")
		p Set the #[code lang] attribute on the #[code html] element, such as #[code lang="en"]. Screen readers use it to read the text in the right language, and Tablechart uses it to format the numbers. See #[a(href=url('/features/numbers.html')) Numbers].
